BinSafe: store oCMobLockable's `locked` and oCMOB's `moveable` as 0xFFFFFFFF like ZenGin.

WriteArchiveBinsafe::write_bool always wrote 1 for true. ZenGin declares those two
flags as signed one-bit bit-fields, so reading a set flag yields -1 and its archiver
stores 0xFFFFFFFF. A census of all 9,802 distinct (class, entry) BOOL keys across the
three retail Gothic II BinSafe worlds (NewWorld, OldWorld, AddonWorld) finds exactly
three keys with a non-0/1 value, and they never use 1 for true: oCMobContainer.locked
(199), oCMobDoor.locked (31) and oCMobSwitch.moveable (5). The quirk is therefore keyed
on the entry name. `takeable` sits in the same bit-field group but is false in every
retail world, so no evidence exists either way and it is left writing 1. The ASCII
writer already emits "1" there and is left alone.

diff --git a/src/archive/ArchiveBinsafe.cc b/src/archive/ArchiveBinsafe.cc
index f3aa39d..d81511f 100644
--- a/src/archive/ArchiveBinsafe.cc
+++ b/src/archive/ArchiveBinsafe.cc
@@ -351,7 +351,15 @@ namespace zenkit {
 
 	void WriteArchiveBinsafe::write_bool(std::string_view name, bool v) {
 		this->write_entry(name, ArchiveEntryType::BOOL);
-		this->_m_write->write_uint(v ? 1 : 0);
+
+		// Quirk: ZenGin declares oCMobLockable's `locked` and oCMOB's `moveable` as signed
+		// one-bit bit-fields, so reading a set flag yields -1 and its archiver stores
+		// 0xFFFFFFFF rather than 1. Every other BOOL in the retail worlds is 0 or 1.
+		if (v && (name == "locked" || name == "moveable")) {
+			this->_m_write->write_uint(0xFFFFFFFFu);
+		} else {
+			this->_m_write->write_uint(v ? 1 : 0);
+		}
 	}
 
 	void WriteArchiveBinsafe::write_color(std::string_view name, Color v) {
